WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:03.689
For the first time ever, this week's guest video
uses art and animation.

00:00:03.689 --> 00:00:05.680
12tone is a channel about music theory

00:00:05.680 --> 00:00:09.470
that uses pen and paper,
and piano notes, and doodles of elephants,

00:00:09.470 --> 00:00:12.820
but today they are also talking about language.

00:00:12.820 --> 00:00:13.920
12tone: [beeps]!

00:00:13.920 --> 00:00:18.590
Hey, welcome to 12tone!
Or, to put that another way: [piano notes]!

00:00:18.590 --> 00:00:21.650
Ok, that may have sounded like
a nice but strange melody,

00:00:21.650 --> 00:00:24.430
but it was actually a sentence in
La Langue Musicale Universelle,

00:00:24.430 --> 00:00:27.240
or the Universal Musical Language,
also known as Solresol.

00:00:27.240 --> 00:00:32.210
Solresol was invented in the mid-19th century
by a French musician named Jean-François Sudre,

00:00:32.210 --> 00:00:34.829
who'd become fascinated with the idea of a
language made of music.

00:00:34.829 --> 00:00:37.790
His first attempt, which he called La Telephonie,
was fairly straightforward:

00:00:37.790 --> 00:00:41.070
He just took the French alphabet and assigned
each letter to a musical note.

00:00:41.070 --> 00:00:42.800
This wasn't really a new language, though.

00:00:42.800 --> 00:00:44.040
It was more like a code,

00:00:44.040 --> 00:00:46.760
and it found its home where most codes do:
the military.

00:00:46.760 --> 00:00:50.060
Sudre tried to sell his system to the
French Army and, later, the Navy,

00:00:50.060 --> 00:00:53.210
but despite early interest,
they never actually paid him for it.

00:00:53.210 --> 00:00:55.559
He even built a series of properly-tuned cannons

00:00:55.559 --> 00:00:58.880
in order to increase the volume of his transmissions,
but to no avail.

00:00:58.880 --> 00:01:01.020
Still, though, he wasn't about to
give up on his dream.

00:01:01.020 --> 00:01:03.740
Instead, he set his sights on an
even more ambitious task:

00:01:03.740 --> 00:01:05.929
A universal second language.

00:01:05.929 --> 00:01:09.159
His goal was to create a language that was
bound to no particular country,

00:01:09.159 --> 00:01:11.759
one that every person learned in addition
to their native tongue,

00:01:11.759 --> 00:01:14.729
allowing free communication
across cultural borders.

00:01:14.729 --> 00:01:18.000
But just converting French into a
musical code wasn't good enough for that.

00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:20.630
Sudre was going to have to invent a real language.

00:01:20.630 --> 00:01:23.740
To do this, he turned to a system of
musical notation known as solfège.

00:01:23.740 --> 00:01:26.759
This is that Do-Re-Mi stuff Julie Andrews
was singing about.

00:01:26.759 --> 00:01:28.840
Well, almost: In The Sound Of Music,
the notes are

00:01:28.840 --> 00:01:31.829
Do, Re, Mi, Fa, Sol, La, Ti, Do, which is
the English method,

00:01:31.829 --> 00:01:34.520
but most other countries in Europe
use "Si" instead,

00:01:34.520 --> 00:01:36.979
and since Sudre was French,
that's what he did.

00:01:36.979 --> 00:01:40.860
Anyway, having so few possible syllables presents
a fairly obvious problem.

00:01:40.860 --> 00:01:44.240
In English, it's hard to even guess how many
single-syllable words there are,

00:01:44.240 --> 00:01:47.130
but one estimate I found
puts it at roughly 7,000.

00:01:47.130 --> 00:01:49.999
Solresol, on the other hand, has 7.

00:01:49.999 --> 00:01:52.770
These are reserved for only the most basic
elements of communication,

00:01:52.770 --> 00:01:55.360
like "yes", "no", "and", and "the".

00:01:55.360 --> 00:01:58.170
Two-syllable words aren't much better: there's
49 possible combinations,

00:01:58.170 --> 00:02:01.950
used for things like pronouns, greetings,
and common descriptors like "good" and "bad".

00:02:01.950 --> 00:02:03.429
They're also used for conjugation:

00:02:03.429 --> 00:02:06.259
Solresol can't fit a bunch of different words
for each verb,

00:02:06.259 --> 00:02:08.460
so instead it has one verb form, the present,

00:02:08.460 --> 00:02:11.650
and then just uses a set of two-syllable words
to tell you about the tense.

00:02:11.650 --> 00:02:13.819
But where things get really interesting are
the larger words,

00:02:13.819 --> 00:02:15.840
which have the room to build in some structures.

00:02:15.840 --> 00:02:19.790
Three-syllable words are loosely divided into
groups of 6, based on the first two notes.

00:02:19.790 --> 00:02:22.150
Words starting with Sol-Si are based on
fun and games,

00:02:22.150 --> 00:02:25.350
including "run", "laugh", and "smile".

00:02:25.350 --> 00:02:27.040
Words starting with Re-La, on the other hand,

00:02:27.040 --> 00:02:31.020
are negative traits like
"misanthropy", "distrust", and "intolerance".

00:02:31.020 --> 00:02:34.329
At four syllables, he takes this concept to
a whole new level,

00:02:34.329 --> 00:02:37.269
breaking all the words up into seven groups,
called keys,

00:02:37.269 --> 00:02:38.469
based on their first syllable.

00:02:38.469 --> 00:02:41.129
The key of Do, for instance, is
all about humanity,

00:02:41.129 --> 00:02:42.849
including the body, mind, and spirit.

00:02:42.849 --> 00:02:45.500
The key of Re is for words
about the household and family,

00:02:45.500 --> 00:02:48.689
Mi is for actions and flaws,
Fa is for travel and war,

00:02:48.689 --> 00:02:51.420
Sol is for art and science,
La is for industry,

00:02:51.420 --> 00:02:52.900
and Si is for government.

00:02:52.900 --> 00:02:54.380
There's also a second set of keys
that come up

00:02:54.380 --> 00:02:56.810
when your word includes a note
repeated twice in a row,

00:02:56.810 --> 00:02:59.400
although their meanings are usually related
to the main key.

00:02:59.400 --> 00:03:00.549
And that's it.

00:03:00.549 --> 00:03:02.299
Sudre's language only went up to 4 syllables,

00:03:02.299 --> 00:03:04.260
with less than 3000 total words.

00:03:04.260 --> 00:03:07.600
The fifth syllable was left open for future
development, in case new words were needed,

00:03:07.600 --> 00:03:10.500
but by carefully removing things like
synonyms, tense, and parts of speech,

00:03:10.500 --> 00:03:13.670
which are managed exclusively through accents
rather than new words,

00:03:13.670 --> 00:03:16.740
he managed to squeeze quite a lot of meaning
into just those 3,000.

00:03:16.740 --> 00:03:20.170
Sadly, while Sudre's work made him famous,
at least in certain circles,

00:03:20.170 --> 00:03:22.780
it never repaid him for the time and money
he put into it.

00:03:22.780 --> 00:03:24.200
To quote the writer Paul Collins,

00:03:24.200 --> 00:03:27.230
"Solresol is, at heart, the philanthropic
effort of an idealist –

00:03:27.230 --> 00:03:30.930
"and the Brotherhood of Mankind does not issue
quarterly dividend checks."

00:03:30.930 --> 00:03:33.520
After Sudre's death, though, his wife Josephine
took up the cause,

00:03:33.520 --> 00:03:35.250
and the language started to pick up steam,

00:03:35.250 --> 00:03:38.860
with thousands of speakers and a society for
its preservation founded in Paris,

00:03:38.860 --> 00:03:41.639
culminating in 1902 with the publication of
a guide to its grammar

00:03:41.639 --> 00:03:44.080
by the head of the society, Boleslas Gajewski.

00:03:44.080 --> 00:03:46.640
But the dream of a true universal language
was fading,

00:03:46.640 --> 00:03:49.959
so preservationists focused on another of
Sudre's selling points: accessibility.

00:03:49.959 --> 00:03:53.529
You see, Solresol doesn't actually have to
be based on music.

00:03:53.529 --> 00:03:56.890
It's got seven characters, and can be grafted
onto any set of seven items,

00:03:56.890 --> 00:04:01.249
like the seven colors of the rainbow or,
more importantly, seven specific hand gestures.

00:04:01.249 --> 00:04:02.980
This meant that anyone who knew
the spoken language

00:04:02.980 --> 00:04:05.249
could easily learn it
as a sign language as well,

00:04:05.249 --> 00:04:08.420
making it an incredibly useful tool in communicating
with deaf people.

00:04:08.420 --> 00:04:12.090
Gajewski's guide emphasized this possibility,
but there was a problem:

00:04:12.090 --> 00:04:15.970
At the time, deaf education in Europe was
dominated by a philosophy called oralism,

00:04:15.970 --> 00:04:18.820
which is basically the idea that deaf people
should still learn to speak,

00:04:18.820 --> 00:04:21.220
and that teaching them sign languages
got in the way.

00:04:21.220 --> 00:04:25.039
To be clear, this is no longer considered
a reasonable approach, but for Gajewski,

00:04:25.039 --> 00:04:28.720
it meant that the language he loved was cut
off from the people it could help the most.

00:04:28.720 --> 00:04:31.389
So who knows, maybe if turn-of-the-century
educators in France

00:04:31.389 --> 00:04:33.030
had been a bit more forward-thinking,

00:04:33.030 --> 00:04:34.750
we'd all be speaking in notes right now.

00:04:34.750 --> 00:04:35.810
Probably not.

00:04:35.810 --> 00:04:37.370
But maybe.

00:04:37.370 --> 00:04:38.930
[piano notes]

00:04:38.930 --> 00:04:40.490
12tone: [beeps]!

00:04:40.490 --> 00:04:42.539
Go subscribe to them for more videos
like that one,

00:04:42.539 --> 00:04:47.020
I would recommend starting with their video
about the Imperial March from Star Wars.

00:04:47.020 --> 00:04:50.680
Next week: the last guest video,
which features a Canadian.

00:04:53.120 --> 00:04:57.460
[beeps]

